From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tiberiu Chibici <chibicitiberiu@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 20:26:16 +0300
Subject: mesa: texture compression at upload for every game (NV30_FORCE_DXT,
 off by default)

Most games upload plain RGB(A)8 textures; on the PS3 those cost 4 bytes a texel of VRAM (RGB is
stored as X8R8G8B8), and VRAM is also the swap tier. NV30_FORCE_DXT=opaque stores a glTexImage2D
of GL_TEXTURE_2D level 0 with an internal format GL_RGB, GL_RGB8, 3, GL_RGBA, GL_RGBA8 or 4 from
8-bit RGB/BGR/RGBA/BGRA data (unsigned byte or the 8888 packed types) as DXT1 when it has no alpha
(an RGB internal format or source, or every alpha byte 255, scanned eight bytes at a time and
stopping at the first other value): an eighth of the VRAM and of the upload. NV30_FORCE_DXT=all
also stores images with alpha as DXT5 (a quarter). The image gets a DXT TexFormat while its
InternalFormat stays the game's, and Mesa's S3TC texstore compresses it as for any compressed
upload: through libspeq on the PS3 (0011, 0032; the SPEs take RGB and RGBA sources directly).
Levels above 0 follow level 0 through _mesa_choose_texture_format's choice from the level below.

Not taken: glTexImage2D(NULL) without a PBO (render targets, textures filled by glTexSubImage),
smaller than NV30_FORCE_DXT_MIN (64) in width or height, sizes not powers of two (nv30 lays DXT
out for POT mipmaps; its NPOT layout with DXT is untested), borders, pixel transfer ops, swapped
bytes, textures whose magnification filter is already GL_NEAREST when uploaded (pixel art and UI
drawn 1:1 show DXT's blocks most; NV30_FORCE_DXT_NEAREST=1 takes them), cube maps (every face
would have to decide alike), other targets, formats and types, drivers without native DXT1/DXT5,
and a level 0 whose texture already has an uncompressed level 1 of the same internal format
(levels uploaded smallest first, or a chain made with glTexImage2D(NULL) and given data later:
every level must have one format, or the texture is incomplete).

Afterwards the texture behaves as the game uploaded it:
- glTexSubImage on whole 4x4 blocks (a partial block only at the right or bottom edge): those
  blocks are compressed again from the new pixels; any other update, glCopyTexSubImage and a
  framebuffer attachment first turn every compressed-at-upload level back into the format the
  game asked for (every level decoded first with st_GetTexSubImage, a GPU blit on nv30, then
  each specified again from level 0 up, the game's unpack state set aside), once per texture;
  the error checks then see an uncompressed texture.
- glGenerateMipmap and GL_GENERATE_MIPMAP take Mesa's path for compressed textures (the base
  read back decoded, the levels made on the CPU and compressed: libspeq again); timed.
- glGetTexImage returns the decoded blocks, GL_TEXTURE_INTERNAL_FORMAT reports the game's
  internal format (texparam.c; not the DXT enum) and GL_TEXTURE_COMPRESSED tells the truth.

NV30_FORCE_DXT_STATS=N prints a "nv30-forcedxt:" line every N compressed textures, at every
return to uncompressed, at context destruction and at exit (textures, DXT1/DXT5, levels, KiB before and
after, sub-image updates, returns to uncompressed, mipmap generations and their ms, the alpha
scan's ms, and the textures kept by reason); NV30_FORCE_DXT_DEBUG=1 a line per decision. The
decision is plain C in texforcedxt_policy.h, tested on its own on the host. The switches say NV30
(the PS3's are NV30_*), but this is core Mesa: softpipe takes the same paths (the host check).

Forwarded: no

